# RunicAI Standard Coding Agent

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"},
  {:runic_ai, path: "~/wrk/runic_ai"}
])

Code.require_file("support/runic_ai_livebook.exs", __DIR__)
```

## What This Notebook Teaches

`RunicAI.Coding.Agent` is the direct local coding agent. It is best when one
agent can inspect, patch, and verify a workspace through explicit tools.

It is good at:

* repository questions and diff reviews
* focused edits with structured patch evidence
* targeted verification loops
* local safety controls through command policy and human review
* runtime-owned state, traces, snapshots, and resume points

It is not the planner. It does not recursively design new workflows by itself.
When you need planning, decomposition, or child agent orchestration, use the
RLM coding agent and let it call the standard agent as a child workflow.

## Optional Provider Setup

The main cells below use deterministic fake LLM responses, so they do not need
an API key. Use this setup only for the final live-model smoke cell.

```elixir
setup = RunicAI.Livebook.Setup.inputs()
```

## Build A Demo Workspace

The coding agent always works through a `RunicAI.Coding.Workspace`. The
workspace holds the root path, path policy, detected profile, optional
persistence, and metadata used by tools and prompts.

```elixir
alias RunicAI.Coding.{Agent, Workspace}

root = RunicAI.Livebook.CodingDemo.sample_project!("standard-coding-agent")
workspace = Workspace.new!(root: root)

%{workspace_root: workspace.root, profile: workspace.profile.name}
```

## Where It Fits

```elixir
Kino.DataTable.new([
  %{
    need: "Explain a repo or review a diff",
    use: "Agent.read_only/3 or command: :review",
    reason: "Read/search/git-read tools only"
  },
  %{
    need: "Make a scoped edit",
    use: "Agent.patch/3",
    reason: "Adds apply_patch while retaining review gates"
  },
  %{
    need: "Repair and verify",
    use: "Agent.verify/3",
    reason: "Adds compile, test, formatter, assertions, and command tools"
  },
  %{
    need: "Plan a multi-agent workflow",
    use: "RunicAI.Coding.RLM.Agent",
    reason: "Coordinator proposes, validates, and compiles a workflow graph"
  }
])
```

## Tool Profiles

Modes are conservative tool-visibility presets. The registry may contain more
tools than the model can see; the selected mode controls the visible subset.

```elixir
Kino.DataTable.new(
  Enum.map([:read_only, :patch, :verify], fn mode ->
    %{
      mode: inspect(mode),
      max_turns: RunicAI.Coding.Recipe.named(mode).max_turns,
      visible_tools: Enum.join(Agent.visible_tools(mode), ", ")
    }
  end)
)
```

```elixir
registry = Agent.default_registry(workspace, mode: :verify)
Kino.DataTable.new(RunicAI.Livebook.CodingDemo.tool_rows(registry))
```

## The Agent Loop

This is the loop owned by `RunicAI.Recipes.IntegratedToolAgent`. The model can
request tools, but the workflow chooses visible tools, checks budget and guards,
executes approved work, appends tool observations, and resumes from review or
wait states.

```elixir
Kino.Mermaid.new("""
flowchart TD
  user["User signal"] --> kernel["Integrated agent kernel"]
  kernel --> materialize["Materialize turn request"]
  materialize --> select["Select visible tools"]
  select --> attach["Attach tool schemas"]
  attach --> prompt["Assemble prompt"]
  prompt --> llm["LLM turn"]
  llm --> interpret["Interpret answer or tool calls"]
  interpret -->|answer| answer_budget["Pre-answer budget"]
  answer_budget --> answer_guard["Pre-answer guard"]
  answer_guard --> commit["Commit answer"]
  interpret -->|tool requests| plan["Plan tool execution"]
  plan --> tool_budget["Pre-tool budget"]
  tool_budget --> tool_guard["Review gate"]
  tool_guard -->|allow| execute["Execute tools"]
  tool_guard -->|review or wait| pending["Pending work"]
  pending --> resume["ReviewDecision or ResumeSignal"]
  resume --> execute
  execute --> observe["Append observations"]
  observe --> kernel
""")
```

## Visualize The Actual Workflow

`RunicAI.Coding.Agent.new/2` builds the workflow without running a turn. The
rendered workflow shows the reusable integrated kernel shape; each submitted
signal flows through the same processor.

```elixir
workflow_shape =
  Agent.new(workspace,
    mode: :verify,
    model: "test:model",
    client: RunicAI.Livebook.CodingDemo.text_client("shape only")
  )

RunicAI.Livebook.WorkflowViz.tabs(workflow_shape)
```

## Read-Only Repository QA

This deterministic client asks for `find_files`, then `read_file`, then returns
an answer. The summary exposes the reply, visible tools, tools used, trace
events, and any risks.

```elixir
read_workflow =
  Agent.read_only(workspace, "Where is Demo.answer/0?",
    model: "test:model",
    client: RunicAI.Livebook.CodingDemo.read_only_client()
  )

Agent.summary(read_workflow)
```

```elixir
Kino.Layout.tabs(
  Summary: Kino.Tree.new(Agent.summary(read_workflow)),
  "Trace events": Kino.Tree.new(Agent.trace_events(read_workflow)),
  Runtime: Kino.Tree.new(Agent.runtime_state(read_workflow)),
  Emissions: Kino.Tree.new(Agent.emissions(read_workflow))
)
```

## Patch Mode

Patch mode adds the structured `apply_patch` tool. A patch result records
changed paths, operation summaries, diagnostics, and before/after hashes.

```elixir
patch_root = RunicAI.Livebook.CodingDemo.sample_project!("standard-coding-agent-patch")
patch_workspace = Workspace.new!(root: patch_root)

patch_workflow =
  Agent.patch(patch_workspace, "Change Demo.answer/0 to return 42.",
    model: "test:model",
    client: RunicAI.Livebook.CodingDemo.patch_client(42)
  )

Kino.Layout.tabs(
  Summary: Kino.Tree.new(Agent.summary(patch_workflow)),
  Source: Kino.Text.new(File.read!(Path.join(patch_root, "lib/demo.ex")))
)
```

## HITL Review Gate

The default coding review gate allows ordinary reads and safe patches, but it
pauses before risky work such as deletes, hidden-file writes, shell/process
operations, formatter writes, broad patches, and mutating git commands.

```elixir
Kino.Mermaid.new("""
sequenceDiagram
  participant Model
  participant Workflow
  participant ReviewGate
  participant Human
  participant Tool

  Model->>Workflow: apply_patch delete .env
  Workflow->>ReviewGate: GuardRequest at :pre_tool
  ReviewGate-->>Workflow: ReviewDecision required
  Workflow-->>Human: ReviewRequest with exact tool call
  Human->>Workflow: approve, reject, edit, or cancel
  Workflow->>Tool: execute approved or rewritten plan
  Tool-->>Workflow: PatchResult observation
  Workflow->>Model: observation in next turn
""")
```

```elixir
review_root =
  "standard-coding-agent-review"
  |> RunicAI.Livebook.CodingDemo.sample_project!()
  |> RunicAI.Livebook.CodingDemo.add_old_file!()

review_workspace = Workspace.new!(root: review_root)

paused =
  Agent.patch(review_workspace, "Remove the obsolete lib/old.ex file.",
    model: "test:model",
    client: RunicAI.Livebook.CodingDemo.destructive_patch_client()
  )

review_request = Agent.latest_review_request(paused)
RunicAI.Livebook.CodingDemo.review_summary(review_request)
```

```elixir
alias Runic.Workflow
alias RunicAI.Coding.Review

resumed =
  Workflow.react_until_satisfied(paused, %{
    review_decision: Review.allow(review_request)
  })

%{
  old_file_exists?: File.exists?(Path.join(review_root, "lib/old.ex")),
  summary: Agent.summary(resumed)
}
```

## Sessions And Snapshots

`RunicAI.Coding.Session` adapts the same agent to the runtime harness. Use it
when your application wants durable snapshots, trace capture, interruption
summaries, or a `RunicAI.Runtime.SessionSpec`.

```elixir
alias RunicAI.Coding.Session

session =
  Session.new(workspace,
    mode: :read_only,
    model: "test:model",
    client: RunicAI.Livebook.CodingDemo.text_client("Demo.answer/0 returns 41.")
  )
  |> Session.submit(%{agent_id: "coding", user: "Explain Demo.answer/0."})

Kino.Tree.new(Session.snapshot(session))
```

## Optional Live-Model Smoke

Run this only after setting `OPENAI_API_KEY` in the setup cell.

```elixir
RunicAI.Livebook.Setup.require_openai_key!()

model = RunicAI.Livebook.Setup.read_model!(setup)
llm_opts =
  RunicAI.Livebook.Setup.llm_opts(
    model,
    max_output_tokens: RunicAI.Livebook.Setup.read_max_output_tokens(setup)
  )

live_workflow =
  Agent.read_only(workspace, "Explain the Demo module and cite the file you inspected.",
    model: model,
    llm_opts: llm_opts
  )

Agent.summary(live_workflow)
```

## Design Takeaways

Use the standard coding agent when one workflow-owned loop is enough. Pick the
smallest mode that exposes the needed tools, keep command policy narrow, keep
review gates on for risky local effects, and use summaries plus trace events as
the application-facing evidence of what happened.
